{
  "paraId": "1498B4B7",
  "style": "",
  "text": "Apoptosis and unrepaired DNA damage are considered the principal mechanisms of cell death following IR in solid tumors [33, 34]. By contrast, IR-induced cell-cycle arrest generally acts to promote cell survival rather than cell death after radiation [35]. Taken together, our results strongly suggest that NS1-BP downregulation in ESCC confers radioresistance by enabling cells to evade apoptosis and by sustaining IR-induced G2/M cell-cycle arrest. Perconti and colleagues [36] reported that NS1-BP inhibits HeLa cell proliferation and suppresses c-Myc at the transcriptional level. In line with this, we found that ectopic overexpression of NS1-BP significantly reduced c-Myc protein levels, most likely through direct binding of NS1-BP to the c-Myc promoter sequence. In clinical ESCC specimens, we likewise observed an inverse correlation between c-Myc expression and NS1-BP levels, and found that high c-Myc expression predicted a poor DSS for ESCC patients. Collectively, these findings identify c-Myc as a downstream effector of NS1-BP."
}

